<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Walter Scott</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1771">1771</a>–<a href="/years/1832">1832</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Sir Walter Scott, 1st Baronet</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sir Walter Scott was a Scottish novelist, poet and historian, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a>, who worked as an advocate, Clerk of Session and Sheriff-Depute of Selkirkshire alongside his writing. He first won readers with narrative poems such as Marmion (1808) and The Lady of the Lake (1810). His Waverley novels (1814–1831) were for nearly a century among the most widely read novels in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and they established the historical novel as a genre of European <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanticism">Romanticism</a>. He was president of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-society">Royal Society</a> of Edinburgh from 1820 to 1832 and became a baronet of Abbotsford in 1820.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/walter-scott.jpg" alt="Walter Scott" width="330" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Henry Raeburn · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sir_Henry_Raeburn_-_Portrait_of_Sir_Walter_Scott.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Walter Scott</b>’s Waverley of 1814 set the pattern of the historical novel, and Lord Byron’s Childe Harold’s Pilgrimage, from 1812, made its author the most famous poet in Europe.</blockquote><a href="/movement/romanticism">Romanticism · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Walter Scott</b> used two stanzas in The Lay of the Last Minstrel (1805), Goethe three in the cathedral scene of Faust (1808), and Oscar Wilde wrote a sonnet on hearing it sung in the Sistine Chapel (1881).</blockquote><a href="/songs/dies-irae">Dies irae</a></li><li><blockquote>Child quotes a letter of 8 August 1802 from Charles Kirkpatrick Sharpe to <b>Walter Scott</b>, saying that Sharpe had it from Miss Erskine of Alva, who had written it down from the recitation of an old woman at Alva; so it was already known in Scotland by then.</blockquote><a href="/songs/the-three-ravens">The Three Ravens</a></li></ul></section>
